1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,510
Imagine you have a crazy idea.

2
00:00:03,030 --> 00:00:04,790
You want to build your own satellite.

3
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:09,190
Not a billion-dollar communications
satellite. Something small.

4
00:00:10,040 --> 00:00:12,440
A box about the size of a loaf of bread.

5
00:00:12,960 --> 00:00:19,340
Inside: a computer, solar panels, a
radio, a few sensors. Maybe even a camera.

6
00:00:20,180 --> 00:00:24,140
You build it in your garage. And then
somehow... you put it into orbit.

7
00:00:24,370 --> 00:00:26,760
Sounds impossible. But it isn't.

8
00:00:27,254 --> 00:00:29,654
People have been doing almost
exactly this for years.

9
00:00:29,884 --> 00:00:32,014
They're called CubeSats.

10
00:00:34,347 --> 00:00:35,847
And the surprising part is that the

11
00:00:36,077 --> 00:00:38,107
hardest thing isn't
building the satellite.

12
00:00:38,427 --> 00:00:40,207
It's everything else.

13
00:00:41,687 --> 00:00:45,507
The basic unit is called 1U —
roughly a ten-centimetre cube.

14
00:00:46,307 --> 00:00:50,227
Stack units together and you
get 2U, 3U, 6U and larger.

15
00:00:51,277 --> 00:00:56,337
The idea is simple. Instead of designing
a new spacecraft from scratch, you

16
00:00:56,567 --> 00:00:59,977
reuse standardised sizes
and standardised interfaces.

17
00:01:00,857 --> 00:01:02,517
And suddenly, space is open to

18
00:01:02,747 --> 00:01:05,757
universities, start-ups,
and maybe even you.

19
00:01:07,057 --> 00:01:10,537
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

20
00:01:11,157 --> 00:01:12,757
It really helps the channel.

21
00:01:15,147 --> 00:01:17,847
So what would our little
satellite actually contain?

22
00:01:18,607 --> 00:01:20,207
First, it needs a computer.

23
00:01:20,927 --> 00:01:25,207
Something has to run the software, control
the hardware, and decide what to do.

24
00:01:25,797 --> 00:01:29,417
Then there's power. Solar
panels make electricity.

25
00:01:30,337 --> 00:01:33,777
Batteries store it for when the
satellite slips into Earth's shadow.

26
00:01:34,407 --> 00:01:37,727
Then communication. A radio. An antenna.

27
00:01:37,807 --> 00:01:41,047
And, back on Earth, a
ground station listening.

28
00:01:41,447 --> 00:01:43,147
Then sensors. Temperature.

29
00:01:43,227 --> 00:01:47,187
Voltage. Current.
Acceleration. Magnetic field.

30
00:01:47,417 --> 00:01:50,607
And some way of telling which
way the satellite is pointing.

31
00:01:51,567 --> 00:01:54,667
Because in orbit, saying
"up" is surprisingly hard.

32
00:01:56,877 --> 00:01:59,497
But before we build one,
there's an awkward question.

33
00:02:00,237 --> 00:02:03,897
How much does this little
box cost? The answer is...

34
00:02:03,977 --> 00:02:05,997
more than it looks like it should.

35
00:02:06,817 --> 00:02:09,397
A simple 1U CubeSat
can easily cost tens of

36
00:02:09,627 --> 00:02:15,797
thousands of dollars — just in flight
hardware. The power system. Solar panels.

37
00:02:16,177 --> 00:02:20,357
Batteries. The computer. The radio.
The attitude control. The structure.

38
00:02:20,437 --> 00:02:21,637
And the payload.

39
00:02:22,107 --> 00:02:24,367
Then come the things that
aren't on the parts list.

40
00:02:24,447 --> 00:02:27,647
Testing. Integration. Documentation.

41
00:02:27,877 --> 00:02:29,867
Ground-station operations.

42
00:02:30,097 --> 00:02:33,567
Licensing. And finally... the launch.

43
00:02:34,397 --> 00:02:37,357
A rideshare for a tiny CubeSat
can cost tens of thousands.

44
00:02:37,587 --> 00:02:42,257
Bigger missions and bigger CubeSats
reach hundreds of thousands.

45
00:02:43,057 --> 00:02:45,137
And that's just getting
the thing into space.

46
00:02:47,777 --> 00:02:50,237
And now an even more ridiculous problem.

47
00:02:50,817 --> 00:02:52,457
Remember all those parts?

48
00:02:53,137 --> 00:02:56,437
How on earth does all of
that fit inside one tiny box?

49
00:02:58,187 --> 00:03:01,407
In space, there's no room for
a random pile of electronics.

50
00:03:02,487 --> 00:03:07,727
Every centimetre matters. Every
gram matters. Every watt matters.

51
00:03:08,547 --> 00:03:09,947
The satellite is a three-dimensional

52
00:03:10,027 --> 00:03:12,787
puzzle that has to
survive a rocket launch.

53
00:03:15,257 --> 00:03:17,137
So where do you get all these parts?

54
00:03:17,837 --> 00:03:19,917
You don't have to design
everything yourself.

55
00:03:20,837 --> 00:03:24,057
There's a whole industry selling
components for small satellites:

56
00:03:24,297 --> 00:03:28,277
computers, power systems, radios,
antennas and solar panels.

57
00:03:29,097 --> 00:03:32,757
There are even marketplaces built
specifically for CubeSat parts.

58
00:03:33,737 --> 00:03:35,857
And the parts are standardised, too.

59
00:03:36,597 --> 00:03:39,617
Many share one board format
and simply stack together,

60
00:03:39,697 --> 00:03:42,717
so pieces from different
makers fit into one unit.

61
00:03:43,137 --> 00:03:47,457
Companies like EnduroSat and GomSpace
sell them, and a single subsystem

62
00:03:47,687 --> 00:03:51,517
can cost anywhere from a few thousand
to tens of thousands of dollars.

63
00:03:52,517 --> 00:03:54,297
And these aren't exotic processors.

64
00:03:55,417 --> 00:03:58,837
One current CubeSat computer
uses an ARM Cortex-M7,

65
00:03:58,917 --> 00:04:03,757
with familiar interfaces
— SPI, I²C, UART and CAN.

66
00:04:04,637 --> 00:04:06,717
And it isn't a custom space chip.

67
00:04:07,077 --> 00:04:10,557
It's a real, off-the-shelf board
you could hold in your hand.

68
00:04:11,497 --> 00:04:13,857
A spacecraft needs something
more important than raw

69
00:04:14,087 --> 00:04:17,937
speed: predictable behaviour,
low power and reliability.

70
00:04:18,937 --> 00:04:23,797
More demanding missions can use ARM
Cortex-A processors — and even Linux.

71
00:04:24,897 --> 00:04:26,097
But it isn't fast.

72
00:04:26,957 --> 00:04:30,837
A typical CubeSat computer runs
at a couple of hundred megahertz.

73
00:04:31,197 --> 00:04:35,037
Your laptop runs at four or five
gigahertz, with many more cores.

74
00:04:35,657 --> 00:04:38,537
It trades raw speed for
using almost no power.

75
00:04:39,397 --> 00:04:42,017
And if you're a programmer, this
is where it gets interesting.

76
00:04:42,897 --> 00:04:45,377
You don't write some
mysterious "space language".

77
00:04:45,797 --> 00:04:47,617
You write embedded software.

78
00:04:47,697 --> 00:04:50,297
Often in C or C++.

79
00:04:51,347 --> 00:04:54,347
It might run directly on the
hardware, or on a real-time

80
00:04:54,427 --> 00:04:58,807
operating system like
FreeRTOS, Zephyr or RTEMS.

81
00:04:59,467 --> 00:05:01,567
There are even open frameworks
built for spacecraft.

82
00:05:01,797 --> 00:05:05,567
NASA's F Prime, for example,
is a flight-software

83
00:05:05,647 --> 00:05:08,487
framework for embedded
and spaceflight systems.

84
00:05:09,647 --> 00:05:12,167
So your code might look
something like this.

85
00:05:13,657 --> 00:05:16,417
But there's one huge difference
from a normal computer.

86
00:05:17,137 --> 00:05:19,257
You don't get to SSH into it.

87
00:05:19,497 --> 00:05:21,277
You can't attach a debugger.

88
00:05:21,917 --> 00:05:23,617
And if your software crashes...

89
00:05:24,177 --> 00:05:26,917
your computer is a few
hundred kilometres up.

90
00:05:27,807 --> 00:05:32,367
So it needs watchdogs, safe modes,
fault detection and automatic recovery.

91
00:05:33,267 --> 00:05:36,087
It has to fix some problems
without you there to help.

92
00:05:38,347 --> 00:05:40,987
And one big reason it needs
all that is radiation.

93
00:05:42,027 --> 00:05:46,447
In space, cosmic rays can flip a bit
in memory, or even knock out a chip.

94
00:05:47,047 --> 00:05:50,027
There's no heavy shielding —
that would weigh far too much.

95
00:05:50,647 --> 00:05:53,027
Instead, the computer
watches itself, corrects

96
00:05:53,257 --> 00:05:55,687
errors, and resets when
something goes wrong.

97
00:05:56,727 --> 00:05:58,547
But if you actually want to launch it...

98
00:05:58,627 --> 00:06:00,467
things get serious.

99
00:06:01,287 --> 00:06:04,907
A part that works on your desk isn't
automatically ready for a rocket.

100
00:06:05,587 --> 00:06:10,007
It has to survive vibration, vacuum,
temperature extremes and radiation.

101
00:06:12,677 --> 00:06:16,277
Now, back to that problem of knowing
which way the satellite is pointing.

102
00:06:17,037 --> 00:06:21,637
Picture it tumbling through
space. No floor. No ceiling.

103
00:06:22,177 --> 00:06:24,337
GPS can tell you where you are...

104
00:06:24,417 --> 00:06:26,417
but not which way you're facing.

105
00:06:27,547 --> 00:06:30,307
So satellites combine
gyroscopes, magnetometers

106
00:06:30,537 --> 00:06:33,287
and sun sensors to work
out their attitude.

107
00:06:33,507 --> 00:06:35,767
And then they have to change it.

108
00:06:36,527 --> 00:06:38,127
One common trick is a magnetorquer.

109
00:06:39,359 --> 00:06:43,669
It pushes against Earth's magnetic
field and makes a tiny torque.

110
00:06:43,919 --> 00:06:45,539
It's not a rocket engine.

111
00:06:45,959 --> 00:06:49,199
It doesn't move the
satellite. It just turns it.

112
00:06:49,579 --> 00:06:52,939
And that's enough to point the
satellite exactly where you want.

113
00:06:55,209 --> 00:06:59,549
But none of this is the reason we
built the satellite. We need a mission.

114
00:07:00,009 --> 00:07:06,389
That's the payload. Measure radiation.
Photograph Earth. Study the atmosphere.

115
00:07:06,569 --> 00:07:10,569
Test a new sensor. Or simply
prove a new technology works.

116
00:07:11,539 --> 00:07:16,739
The satellite itself is mostly there
to keep the payload alive. Power it.

117
00:07:16,959 --> 00:07:21,139
Point it. Keep it warm. Store
its data. And send it home.

118
00:07:22,429 --> 00:07:24,849
When your desktop locks
up, you restart it.

119
00:07:25,129 --> 00:07:26,929
When your satellite locks up...

120
00:07:27,209 --> 00:07:30,769
you have a very expensive
object silently orbiting Earth.

121
00:07:33,419 --> 00:07:36,979
So let's say we built it. Everything
works... The computer boots...

122
00:07:37,059 --> 00:07:40,039
The radio talks. The panels
make power. The payload runs...

123
00:07:40,269 --> 00:07:42,779
Can we launch it? ...Not yet.

124
00:07:43,437 --> 00:07:48,377
A satellite has to prove it can
survive the launch. That means testing.

125
00:07:48,877 --> 00:07:50,077
A lot of testing.

126
00:07:50,847 --> 00:07:53,167
First, bolt it to a giant shaker.

127
00:07:53,527 --> 00:07:55,407
Then shake it, hard...

128
00:07:55,707 --> 00:07:57,307
That's vibration testing.

129
00:07:57,847 --> 00:08:00,067
Rocket launches are not gentle.

130
00:08:00,567 --> 00:08:02,927
It has to survive noise,
vibration and shock

131
00:08:03,157 --> 00:08:07,347
without anything coming loose or
breaking. Every screw matters.

132
00:08:07,987 --> 00:08:11,007
Every connector matters.
Every piece of cable matters.

133
00:08:11,837 --> 00:08:15,817
Then comes vacuum. On Earth,
air carries heat away.

134
00:08:16,297 --> 00:08:18,017
In space, there is no air.

135
00:08:18,557 --> 00:08:21,437
One side can get extremely
hot, another extremely cold,

136
00:08:21,667 --> 00:08:24,697
and the electronics still have
to stay inside their limits.

137
00:08:25,397 --> 00:08:28,117
So the satellite needs
thermal straps, coatings,

138
00:08:28,197 --> 00:08:35,221
insulation and carefully chosen materials.
Satellites don't have air conditioning.

139
00:08:35,451 --> 00:08:36,627
They have physics.

140
00:08:36,707 --> 00:08:40,307
And the battery gets interesting.
Charge it with sunlight.

141
00:08:41,187 --> 00:08:42,747
Then the satellite slips into Earth's

142
00:08:42,977 --> 00:08:45,147
shadow, and the panels
make almost nothing.

143
00:08:45,507 --> 00:08:50,927
The battery keeps it alive. Then back
into sunlight. Charge. Discharge. Charge.

144
00:08:51,007 --> 00:08:54,147
Discharge. Again and again. For years.

145
00:08:54,227 --> 00:08:57,567
One mistake in power could
quietly end the mission.

146
00:08:59,987 --> 00:09:04,147
And now, how do you talk to it?
You need a radio. An antenna.

147
00:09:04,727 --> 00:09:08,987
And a frequency you're legally
allowed to use. And a ground station.

148
00:09:09,647 --> 00:09:11,407
The satellite doesn't have the internet.

149
00:09:11,487 --> 00:09:13,787
It just flies overhead and transmits.

150
00:09:14,687 --> 00:09:17,147
Your ground station catches the signal.

151
00:09:17,467 --> 00:09:18,927
The computer decodes it.

152
00:09:19,007 --> 00:09:22,147
And suddenly you have data
from your own spacecraft.

153
00:09:22,767 --> 00:09:25,867
Imagine opening a
terminal and seeing this.

154
00:09:26,247 --> 00:09:28,767
Except the machine
printing those numbers...

155
00:09:29,207 --> 00:09:31,767
is hundreds of kilometres over your head.

156
00:09:33,977 --> 00:09:38,737
So how do we actually get it up there? You
could build a rocket... Please don't...

157
00:09:38,817 --> 00:09:43,537
You don't need one. CubeSats
fly as secondary payloads.

158
00:09:43,976 --> 00:09:47,516
A rocket is already going
up. It has spare room.

159
00:09:48,156 --> 00:09:51,296
Your satellite rides along
in a standard deployer.

160
00:09:51,456 --> 00:09:53,476
No personal rocket required.

161
00:09:54,286 --> 00:09:57,546
And now it's sitting on top of
a rocket. You can't touch it.

162
00:09:57,846 --> 00:09:58,866
You can't open the box.

163
00:09:58,996 --> 00:10:02,446
You can't plug in a cable.
The rocket launches.

164
00:10:03,626 --> 00:10:05,766
About nine minutes later, you're in orbit.

165
00:10:06,546 --> 00:10:08,666
The deployer releases your satellite.

166
00:10:09,306 --> 00:10:11,526
It springs free and begins to tumble.

167
00:10:12,306 --> 00:10:13,813
But how does it know it's out? ...

168
00:10:14,043 --> 00:10:17,286
Spring-loaded switches, pressed
in while it sat in the deployer,

169
00:10:17,516 --> 00:10:20,406
pop free the moment it's
ejected, and the computer boots.

170
00:10:21,246 --> 00:10:24,526
Then, by the rules, it stays
silent for about half an hour.

171
00:10:25,126 --> 00:10:27,186
Only then does it power up its radio,

172
00:10:27,266 --> 00:10:31,766
unfold its antennas, and finally
call home. And somewhere on Earth...

173
00:10:32,186 --> 00:10:33,986
your ground station hears it.

174
00:10:36,596 --> 00:10:40,816
You built a machine. And now that
machine is orbiting the planet.

175
00:10:43,446 --> 00:10:48,426
Except, we're not done. A dead satellite
can't just stay up there forever.

176
00:10:48,966 --> 00:10:51,926
Left in orbit for centuries,
it becomes space debris.

177
00:10:52,956 --> 00:10:54,916
That means modern
missions increasingly have

178
00:10:55,146 --> 00:10:57,476
to plan for the end of
the spacecraft's life.

179
00:10:58,336 --> 00:11:01,656
Ideally, the thin upper
atmosphere slowly drags it down.

180
00:11:01,756 --> 00:11:08,196
Its orbit decays. It re-enters. And
it burns up. The end of the mission...

181
00:11:08,426 --> 00:11:10,276
is part of the mission.

182
00:11:12,976 --> 00:11:17,236
So... could you build your own
satellite? Technically, yes.

183
00:11:17,316 --> 00:11:20,716
You don't need to be NASA. You
don't need to own a rocket.

184
00:11:21,236 --> 00:11:23,836
You don't even necessarily
need a huge company.

185
00:11:24,926 --> 00:11:26,826
The satellite has to survive launch.

186
00:11:26,906 --> 00:11:32,486
Survive space. Communicate
legally. Run on its own.

187
00:11:32,926 --> 00:11:35,186
And, in the end, clear out of orbit.

188
00:11:35,386 --> 00:11:39,006
All of that has to be proven
before anyone puts it on a rocket.

189
00:11:41,496 --> 00:11:43,736
CubeSats didn't make space easy.

190
00:11:44,096 --> 00:11:45,876
They made it standardised.

191
00:11:46,496 --> 00:11:48,616
Instead of building
everything from scratch, you

192
00:11:48,846 --> 00:11:51,496
can build something that
fits an existing standard.

193
00:11:51,636 --> 00:11:54,176
And that's a surprisingly big change.

194
00:11:55,696 --> 00:11:59,376
So somewhere, right now, there
are tiny machines orbiting Earth.

195
00:11:59,976 --> 00:12:03,836
Built by universities, research
teams, companies and students.

196
00:12:05,006 --> 00:12:08,286
Some carry cameras.
Some measure radiation.

197
00:12:08,926 --> 00:12:11,026
Some test brand-new technology.

198
00:12:11,286 --> 00:12:13,606
And some are just experiments asking one

199
00:12:13,836 --> 00:12:16,786
question: "can we make
this thing work in space?"

200
00:12:17,956 --> 00:12:21,176
And that's what makes building
your own satellite so fascinating.

201
00:12:22,056 --> 00:12:25,396
Because the distance between you
and space isn't a rocket anymore.

202
00:12:25,636 --> 00:12:26,576
It might be...

203
00:12:26,806 --> 00:12:28,676
a ten-centimetre box.

204
00:12:28,906 --> 00:12:30,806
And a very, very long checklist.
